WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.520
- Depending on which science
fiction you want to reference,

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.460
the researchers here at the
University of Konstanz in Germany

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.220
have either built a
holodeck from "Star Trek,"

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:09.810
or "The Matrix,"

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.840
or something kind of like
the old alien abductions

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.100
from "The X-Files."

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.670
It's just that they've
built it for locusts.

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.340
- One of the great challenges
in studying collective behavior

00:00:20.340 --> 00:00:22.920
is that there's this intricate
network of interactions

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.610
among the individuals that
we can't see, we can't control.

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.710
And so, it's really
difficult to understand

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.680
how these locusts, with
their local interactions,

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:32.880
can scale up to swarms

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:35.640
that can extend across
hundreds of square kilometers.

00:00:35.640 --> 00:00:38.130
We thought, "What if we
could have individuals

00:00:38.130 --> 00:00:41.520
"moving and interacting
with virtual swarms?"

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.590
And so, that's why we've
designed this setup.

00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.000
What we do is we put the animal

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.180
on this large, motion-compensating sphere.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.320
As the animal moves,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.750
the sphere always moves to keep it on top

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:54.447
and so, it can move in a
never-ending desert environment.

00:00:54.447 --> 00:00:55.920
And by tracking that movement,

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.330
we know where the animal's gone.

00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.030
The locusts believe
these virtual individuals

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.470
to be other locusts.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.960
They will tend to align
their direction of travel

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:05.430
with near individuals.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:09.330
They will think of themselves
as being within a real swarm.

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.450
- Locusts don't have depth perception
in the way that humans do.

00:01:12.450 --> 00:01:15.300
Prey animals generally have
eyes on each side of their head,

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.090
trading off 3D vision for
a much wider field of view.

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.610
So there's no need for
sci-fi holographic systems

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:22.830
or tiny little 3D glasses here.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.980
As long as they keep an exact track
on the locust's eye position

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:27.600
and update the screen fast enough,

00:01:27.600 --> 00:01:29.160
it seems to fool them.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:32.940
I'll be honest, I do find
this a little bit unsettling...

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.940
...and it's only going to get
more unsettling from here.

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:39.000
- Ultimately, decision-making is made
in the brain of the animal.

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:41.640
So if we're to understand
these vast swarms,

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.320
we actually need to go in

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:46.740
and look at how the brain
processes this information.

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:47.573
So in this VR,

00:01:47.573 --> 00:01:49.590
we're basically plugging
into the spinal cord.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:50.940
The animal here is tethered.

00:01:50.940 --> 00:01:54.300
It has to be held in place
but can still walk freely

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.510
on a ball that's elevated by air,
so it doesn't have friction,

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:59.190
allowing the locust, again,

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.950
to move and interact with a virtual swarm.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:04.470
So, we know what the
animal's responding to

00:02:04.470 --> 00:02:07.530
but now we can also
decode the neural signals

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.410
that are underlying the
decisions it's making.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:12.090
So, locusts impact the livelihood

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:15.570
of 1 in 10 people on the
planet through food shortage.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:18.810
They contribute to humanitarian
crises across the globe,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.900
yet no one understood
why they form swarms.

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:23.820
- So, the researchers here
have also commissioned

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.830
this massive imaging hangar

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:28.860
and they're going to track
thousands of locusts individually

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:31.860
by attaching tiny
retroreflective tags to each one

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.890
and using a motion-capture
system with more than 30 cameras.

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.830
Sub-millimeter precision,
100 frames a second.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.420
Again, I had questions,

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.230
starting with: where do you
order thousands of locusts from?

00:02:43.230 --> 00:02:44.280
- Well, luckily for us,

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:47.250
it turns out that
entomophagy or eating insects

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.350
is becoming an important
aspect for humans

00:02:49.350 --> 00:02:51.960
and also for agriculture,
for farm animals,

00:02:51.960 --> 00:02:55.080
so there are actually huge
locust breeding centers.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.410
We collaborate with them and so,
they bred the insects for us.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:01.200
We have a whole team of
experienced individuals(!)

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:03.780
from senior professors to
undergraduate students,

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.070
all working together,

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:07.140
gluing these little
tags on the individuals.

00:03:07.140 --> 00:03:11.070
And this allows us to see the
three-dimensional information

00:03:11.070 --> 00:03:12.480
much, much more easily

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.520
than we could using
many video cameras.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:17.970
The imaging hangar is
15 by 15 by 8 meters.

00:03:17.970 --> 00:03:21.210
It's designed to mimic the environment
in which locusts have evolved.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.830
We have extremely bright lights

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:26.520
that capture the natural wavelength
of light that they experience.

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:32.310
We have the correct temperature of
around 27, 28°C within this large space.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:35.400
So first, we're looking at the locusts
in different densities

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:38.040
to see which different
structures and patterns form,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:39.750
but then, we also want to understand

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:43.080
how these voracious insects
eat their way through crops.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.290
And also, how they avoid
predators when doing so,

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:48.540
so we're also startling the individuals

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.220
to see how social behavior

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:53.310
also influences their
response to predators.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.710
When the insects hatch,
they're flightless.

00:03:55.710 --> 00:03:58.770
In fact, they're flightless for the
first two months of their lives.

00:03:58.770 --> 00:04:02.190
It's only in the final adult stage
that they take to the wing.

00:04:02.190 --> 00:04:03.750
And so, the critical stage

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:06.690
for understanding the swarms
and also controlling them

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:08.880
is these flightless juveniles.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.240
- And this is about 10,000 locusts.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:14.400
There are questions about
testing on insects, of course,

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:16.544
but the consensus right
now is that...

00:04:16.544 --> 00:04:17.857
they're insects.

00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:20.070
And as long as there's
no unnecessary harm caused,

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:22.050
the trade-off is well worth it.

00:04:22.050 --> 00:04:24.090
These would've been ground up
for animal feed, after all,

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.650
and the world doesn't have
a problem with that.

00:04:25.650 --> 00:04:27.960
And locust swarms aren't cooperative.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.990
Researchers have discovered
that locusts march in formation

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:33.690
because traveling in the
same direction at the same speed

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:38.670
is the easiest way for each individual
to avoid being eaten by other locusts.

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:41.310
If humans can understand
this swarm's behavior,

00:04:41.310 --> 00:04:44.640
maybe we can start to understand
the gigantic, devastating swarms

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:46.740
with billions or even trillions
of locusts,

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:49.110
the ones that destroy food supplies.

00:04:49.110 --> 00:04:52.230
Or maybe we can start to
improve the forecasting tools,

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.750
because right now, the
only methods the world has

00:04:54.750 --> 00:04:56.848
to deal with devastating
locust plagues

00:04:56.848 --> 00:05:00.180
are vast amounts of
industrial pesticides...

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:01.350
and hope.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:03.210
- I find it astonishing that
there are pest insects

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.670
that impact so many
people on the planet,

00:05:05.670 --> 00:05:08.130
and yet there's so little
research into this.

00:05:08.130 --> 00:05:09.330
And one of the main reasons

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.930
is that the people that really are
impacted by locusts,

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:14.430
they're subsistence farmers.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.290
They can't afford agrochemicals.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:17.790
You also have countries

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.830
where they've got other
problems to deal with,

00:05:19.830 --> 00:05:22.020
such as internal strife and conflicts.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.300
The last experiments
we did with locusts,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:25.650
when I was based in Oxford,

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.320
we had, I think, the most
was 110 individuals.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:30.270
To approximate what
they're doing in nature,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.390
we need to start looking at
tens of thousands of individuals.

00:05:33.390 --> 00:05:34.560
The key here

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:37.500
is that we need these very
large-scale experiments

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.220
to be that bridge between
the lab and the wild.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:42.690
One of the amazing things about locusts

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:46.170
is that the swarms just
suddenly appear out of nowhere.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:50.970
And so, what we're trying to mimic here is:
as population density increases,

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.340
we think that these swarms
will suddenly emerge.

00:05:53.340 --> 00:05:56.100
They'll suddenly start
marching together in unison.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.650
We're just beginning to understand
the wonders of collectives,

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:01.500
how groups of neurons
communicate with each other

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.570
to allow animals to make decisions,

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:06.810
and then, how social interactions
connect brains together

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.370
such as in locust swarms.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:11.070
So, we think locusts can
sense the environment

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.590
through this communication
within the swarm.

00:06:13.590 --> 00:06:15.360
This gives them a
swarm intelligence

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:18.030
that's beyond the brain
of any individual.

00:06:18.030 --> 00:06:20.340
We can use this to
begin to understand

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:23.213
how and why these swarms form
and therefore,

00:06:23.213 --> 00:06:26.043
how we can best minimize
the impact on humans.

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:28.350
- It's a couple of hours later.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:30.450
The imaging hangar
has been blocked off

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:31.800
from all humans
for a while,

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.710
and the researchers here are
now seeing marching behavior

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.780
that has never been
observed in the lab before.

00:06:36.780 --> 00:06:38.790
It's only ever been in the wild.

00:06:38.790 --> 00:06:40.230
It's working,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:41.850
and they've come up
with like a dozen ideas

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.440
for new experiments in
the last 10 minutes.

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:44.389
This is brilliant.

